---
import StaticPage from "../layouts/StaticPage.astro";

const home = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/ui\/$/, "");
const repository = "https://github.com/simonvanlierde/brightway-benchmarks";
---

<StaticPage
  mainClass="about"
  current="about"
  title="About and privacy · Brightway Benchmarks"
  description="How the Brightway benchmark evidence is produced, what compatibility checks cover, and what this static viewer stores in your browser."
  canonical="https://simonvanlierde.com/brightway-benchmarks/about/"
>
      <h1>About these benchmarks</h1>
      <p class="lede">Performance histories and tested installation sets for the Brightway life cycle assessment stack.</p>

      <h2>What the evidence covers</h2>
      <p>The benchmark cases cover calculations, database writes, graph traversal and time-explicit LCA. Each calculation checks its result against an independent reference. The histories keep releases, environments and machines separate so the comparison stays inspectable.</p>
      <p>Most cases use seeded synthetic databases shaped like ecoinvent. Two real databases run beside them: USEEIO 1.1, a public-domain US EPA database, and BAFU 2025, the Swiss Federal Office for the Environment's inventory database, which is downloaded for each run and never redistributed. The <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/docs/method.md`}>method</a> describes the inputs, reference checks and measurement noise; the <a href={`${home}findings/`}>findings</a> explain the measured changes and their limits.</p>

      <p>The solver story reads the numbers in plain terms: <a href={`${home}stories/solver-choice/`}>your solver choice can change a Brightway score's time by 5 times or more</a>. The <a href={`${home}stories/home-heating/`}>home-heating case</a> shows why checking a linear solve does not validate the inventory or impact factors.</p>

      <h2>What a passing setup means</h2>
      <p>Smoke checks install a package set and run small-database calculations. Upstream-suite checks run the packages' own tests. Dependency floors, pre-releases and conda-forge are separate diagnostic checks. A pass in one check does not establish that every workflow, solver or platform works.</p>
      <p>Setup shows each check's current result and keeps its last passing installation files. Dated files stay fixed; “Copy latest” follows the newest passing set. <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/docs/running.md#install-a-set-that-works`}>Install-set documentation</a> explains the commands and coverage.</p>

      <h2>Source and citation</h2>
      <p>The <a href={repository}>source code and issue tracker</a> are public under the <a href={`${repository}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>BSD 3-Clause license</a>. Published software releases are archived on Zenodo. Use the <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23727602">software citation</a> when you rely on this work.</p>

      <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
      <p>The viewer has no analytics or advertising scripts. It saves your package-manager and theme preferences in this browser's local storage; clear this site's browser storage to remove them. Benchmark selections are kept in the page URL so you can share a view. A search from the About or story pages puts its words in the URL, and the host logs the address of every request.</p>
      <p>The site is hosted on GitHub Pages. GitHub logs and stores visitors' IP addresses for security purposes, as explained in its <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/what-is-github-pages#data-collection">Pages documentation</a> and <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">privacy statement</a>. Following links to GitHub, Zenodo or package documentation opens those services under their own policies.</p>
    </StaticPage>
